# Resolving a node inside a frame — what a read means while imagining.
#
# This is the non-trivial half of the binder. A context established by `workbench.step` names this
# function and points at the frame; the vocabulary in `access.mf` invokes it before every read and every
# write, so a rule bound to a real node reads the frame's version of it without containing one word about
# frames.
#
# Written in the surface rather than as a native, and that is not a preference. Planning that Python owns
# is planning the system cannot inspect or change, which is the island the whole design exists to avoid —
# and resolution is the most load-bearing decision planning makes.
#
# **Reading walks the frame chain.** A frame maps only what changed in it, so *no version here* means
# *unchanged*, and unchanged means whatever the previous frame said. A node nothing has touched resolves
# all the way to frame 0; a node from outside the workbench entirely resolves to itself. That last case
# is what makes this safe to put under every read at once: resolution is idempotent, because the
# vocabulary cannot know whether its caller was handed a real node or an imagined one.
#
# Termination is trivial, and worth contrasting with `path.reaches`, which needs a seen-set because the
# world has cycles. This walks a *history*, and a history does not loop.

# The version of `node` in force in this context's frame — this frame's, the nearest ancestor's, or the
# node itself.
#
# One `DEREF` per frame, because the frame carries an index from identity to the version it holds
# (`workbench.index`). That index is an ordinary stored reference — the substrate's key-to-node map,
# which knows nothing about frames — and this reads exactly the structure Python's `mapping_for` reads,
# so the two walks cannot drift into disagreeing.
#
# What it replaces was a search: ask the reverse index which mappings name this node, then ask each one
# which frame it belongs to. That is O(versions) per read, and ambiguous besides — `execution.bind` points
# at a mapping under the same label a frame does, so the sources of a mapping are its frame *and* every
# replay that has bound it, sorted by id, with `bound` sorting first. An index cannot have that problem.
fn in_frame(node) -> thing:
    NATIVE R(ctx) "context"
    GET R(f) R(ctx) "frame"

    # First, what is this a version OF. A caller may hand over an identity (the canonical node) or a
    # version of it (an image), and both must resolve to the same place — the vocabulary cannot know
    # which it was given, and an edge read inside a frame answers with a version by construction.
    #
    # An imagined node is its own identity: its mapping has no `original`, which is not a missing pointer
    # but a positive fact — *this does not exist yet and must be created when the plan runs* — so it is
    # indexed under itself and the walk finds its first version exactly as a real node's finds frame 0's.
    COPY R(id) F(node)
    NSOURCES R(nv) F(node) "image"
    CONST R(vi) 0
.each_holder:
    LT R(more_v) R(vi) R(nv)
    JMPNOT R(more_v) .walk
    SOURCE_AT R(vm) F(node) "image" R(vi)
    KIND R(vk) R(vm)
    EQ R(v_is_mapping) R(vk) "mapping"
    JMPNOT R(v_is_mapping) .next_holder
    GET R(vo) R(vm) "original"
    JMPNOT R(vo) .walk
    COPY R(id) R(vo)
    JMP .walk
.next_holder:
    ADD R(vi) R(vi) 1
    JMP .each_holder

.walk:
    JMPNOT R(f) .itself
    DEREF R(m) R(f) R(id)
    JMPIF R(m) .found

    # Nothing in this frame: ask the frame it was derived from. `frame -next-> frame` read backwards, and
    # a frame has exactly one predecessor, so the one-element answer has no order to get wrong.
    NSOURCES R(np) R(f) "next"
    JMPNOT R(np) .itself
    SOURCE_AT R(f) R(f) "next" 0
    JMP .walk

.found:
    GET R(result) R(m) "image"
    RET

.itself:
    COPY R(result) R(id)
